  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1227 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe frontal boundary continues to slowly push to the southeast and \nover the Coastal Plains on Thursday evening/early Friday morning. No \nrain anticipated across our area for this period. Otherwise, another \nround of cool airmass spreads across South Central Texas in the wake \nof the front with lows in the 40s and 50s. Breezy northerly winds \nare forecast for Thursday night into the overnight hours. \n\nFriday's high range from the mid to upper 60s across the Hill \nCountry and southern Edwards Plateau to mid 70s over the rest of the \nlocal area. Northerly breezes are forecast for Friday with partly \ncloudy skies.\n\nThe upcoming weekend brings some challenges as far as what to expect.\nThe GEFS solution is faster than the EPS for our next upper level \ntrough and therefore, one brings more showers and storms across the \nlocal area than the other. A blend of these solutions give us rain \nchances of 20 percent across South Central Texas especially from \nmidday Saturday to midday Sunday. The Rio Grande and southern \nEdwards Plateau are favored for getting the best chances for rain. \nQPF values for the period could range from a few hundredths to one \nquarter to half inch. Then, dry and cooler weather returns through \nat least Wednesday of next week in the wake of the weekend cold \nfront passage. Highs over the weekend to range from the low 60s to \nlow 70s with lows in the 40s and 50s. Slow warming early next week \nwith highs reaching low to mid 70s by Tuesday.",
